<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 223 0 R
/T (The C++ engine is usable alone from the terminal. The Python adapter calls the engine as a subprocess \(afterimage index and afterimage search --json\), adds query understanding and hybrid ranking, and serves the browser interface. SQLite is the only place where the indexing path and the search path meet.)
/E (The C++ engine is usable alone from the terminal. The Python adapter calls the engine as a subprocess \(afterimage index and afterimage search --json\), adds query understanding and hybrid ranking, and serves the browser interface. SQLite is the only place where the indexing path and the search path meet.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
833 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 223 0 R
/T (Development approach)
/E (Development approach)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
834 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 223 0 R
/T (The system was built module by module, bottom-up: document model, ingestion, extraction, storage, index, retrieval, ranking, graph, command-line interface, and finally the Python adapter and browser interface. Each module has a public header under include/afterimage/<module>/ and a unit-test file, so it could be tested before the next module depended on it. Derived data \(the index and graph\) is never stored; it is rebuilt from SQLite, so it cannot disagree with the stored text.)
/E (The system was built module by module, bottom-up: document model, ingestion, extraction, storage, index, retrieval, ranking, graph, command-line interface, and finally the Python adapter and browser interface. Each module has a public header under include/afterimage/<module>/ and a unit-test file, so it could be tested before the next module depended on it. Derived data \(the index and graph\) is never stored; it is rebuilt from SQLite, so it cannot disagree with the stored text.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
